  doi: 10.3389/fbinf.2026.1910035
  journal: "Frontiers in Bioinformatics"
  month: 10
  title: "OBLX: building standardized reference resources for reproducible bioinformatics data analysis"
  volume: 6
  year: 2026
  abstract: >-
    Reproducible next-generation sequencing (NGS) analyses in cancer research
    require standardized reference genome resources, yet these resources are
    often assembled inconsistently, limiting the comparability of results across
    pipelines and studies. While existing solutions support a range of workflows,
    they provide only limited documentation of the included data and processing
    steps. Furthermore, some of these resources are not version-matched or
    outdated, making them less suitable for integrated and reproducible cancer
    genomics analyses. To address this gap, we developed the Omics builder for
    Bioinformatics resource Libraries and indeXes (OBLX,
    https://github.com/TRON-Bioinformatics/oblx/), a fully documented,
    configurable and reproducible pipeline for building standardized NGS
    reference libraries. First, the pipeline retrieves genome and transcriptome
    reference sequences, their corresponding annotations, and population variant
    resources from public sources. Subsequently, OBLX processes these resources
    to ensure compatibility with commonly used NGS tools, which includes building
    several bioinformatic indices. The resulting OBLX library may then be directly
    used by downstream tools and pipelines. By documenting all resources,
    operations, and intended use-cases, OBLX improves transparency,
    reproducibility, and interoperability for NGS data analyses.
